<main>
  <!-- PDF page 01; original slide 01 -->
  <section class="slide cover" id="slide-01" aria-labelledby="title-01" data-pdf-page="1" data-original-slide="1">
    <h1 id="title-01">Lecture 09 · The Repressilator</h1>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <p class="thesis">Repression must be strong enough <br>and the response slow enough <br>for an oscillator</p>
      <div class="equation hero" role="math">Re λ<sub>2,3</sub> = g/2 − γ &gt; 0 &nbsp; ⇔ &nbsp; g/γ &gt; 2</div>
      <div class="cover-meta"><p>The local instability threshold of the symmetric three-variable model</p><p>Presenter: Wang Fei</p></div>
    </div>
    
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 01 / 17</span><span>01</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 02; original slide 02 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-02" aria-labelledby="title-02" data-pdf-page="2" data-original-slide="2">
    <h2 id="title-02">The quantitative argument</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="two-columns">
        <div><h3 class="teal">Strong enough</h3><div class="equation teal" role="math">g = −f′(x<sup>∗</sup>)</div><p>Production must respond steeply<br>to upstream changes near x<sup>∗</sup>.</p></div>
        <div><h3 class="amber">Slow enough</h3><div class="equation amber" role="math">τ = 1/γ</div><p>Finite turnover provides memory.<br>Lower γ weakens damping at fixed g.</p></div>
      </div>
      <hr>
    </div>
    
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 02 / 17</span><span>02</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 03; original slide 03 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-03" aria-labelledby="title-03" data-pdf-page="3" data-original-slide="3">
    <h2 id="title-03">Three repressors in a negative-feedback ring</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="two-columns ring-layout">
        <figure><svg class="ring" viewBox="0 0 430 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="ring-title ring-desc">
  <title id="ring-title">Three repressors in a negative feedback ring</title>
  <desc id="ring-desc">A represses B, B represses C, and C represses A. T bars indicate repression.</desc>
  <defs><marker id="repression-bar" viewBox="0 0 14 16" refX="12" refY="8" markerWidth="14" markerHeight="16" orient="auto" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><path d="M12 1V15" fill="none" stroke="#b36a00" stroke-width="2.3"></path></marker></defs>
  <g fill="none" stroke="#b36a00" stroke-width="2.3" marker-end="url(#repression-bar)">
    <path d="M116 63H289"></path><path d="M318 98L234 245"></path><path d="M183 245L99 98"></path>
  </g>
  <g fill="white" stroke-width="2.2"><circle cx="80" cy="63" r="36" stroke="#008a8c"></circle><circle cx="326" cy="63" r="36" stroke="#1266a4"></circle><circle cx="209" cy="280" r="36" stroke="#b36a00"></circle></g>
  <g text-anchor="middle" font-size="32" font-weight="700" font-family="Arial, sans-serif"><text x="80" y="74" fill="#008a8c">A</text><text x="326" y="74" fill="#1266a4">B</text><text x="209" y="291" fill="#a86200">C</text></g>
</svg></figure>
        <div class="spaced-list"><p class="teal"><strong>A high represses B</strong></p><p class="blue"><strong>Low B allows C to rise</strong></p><p class="amber"><strong>High C represses A</strong></p><p class="ring-note">Expression and turnover take time.<br>Previous molecules persist as<br>the next phase develops.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 03 / 17</span><span>03</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 04; original slide 04 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-04" aria-labelledby="title-04" data-pdf-page="4" data-original-slide="4">
    <h2 id="title-04">The reduced biological model</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="equation " role="math">dx<sub>i</sub>/dt = α / (1 + x<sub>i−1</sub><sup>n</sup>) + α<sub>0</sub> − γx<sub>i</sub></div>
      <p>x<sub>i</sub> is a repressor concentration scaled by a common repression threshold.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table ">
      <caption class="sr-only">Parameters in the reduced biological model</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Parameter</th><th scope="col">Biological meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>α, n</td><td>Regulated production range and Hill steepness</td></tr>
      <tr><td>α<sub>0</sub></td><td>Basal leak expression under repression</td></tr>
      <tr><td>γ</td><td>Degradation plus growth dilution</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">The reduced model has one variable per node. The original circuit model separates mRNA and protein.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 04 / 17</span><span>04</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 05; original slide 05 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-05" aria-labelledby="title-05" data-pdf-page="5" data-original-slide="5">
    <h2 id="title-05">Two-stage and three-stage repression rings</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="two-columns">
        <div><h3 class="blue">Two stages</h3><div class="equation " role="math">A ⊣ B ⊣ A</div><div class="equation blue" role="math">(−) × (−) = (+)</div><p>Positive feedback can support<br>mutually exclusive states or<br>bistability.</p></div>
        <div><h3 class="teal">Three stages</h3><div class="equation " role="math">A ⊣ B ⊣ C ⊣ A</div><div class="equation teal" role="math">(−) × (−) × (−) = (−)</div><p>Negative feedback plus response lag<br>can support oscillatory instability.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">Topology supplies the feedback sign. Kinetics and eigenvalues determine the instability.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 05 / 17</span><span>05</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 06; original slide 07 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-07" aria-labelledby="title-07" data-pdf-page="6" data-original-slide="7">
    <h2 id="title-07">Why the simple two-variable model excludes Hopf</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="two-columns hopf-layout"><div><div class="equation " role="math">dx/dt = F(y) − γ<sub>x</sub>x</div><div class="equation " role="math">dy/dt = G(x) − γ<sub>y</sub>y</div></div><div class="matrix-equation"><span>J =</span><div class="matrix-wrap small" role="math" aria-label="Two by two Jacobian: minus gamma x, F prime of y star; G prime of x star, minus gamma y.">
      <span class="matrix-bracket left" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <table class="matrix"><tbody>
        <tr><td>−γ<sub>x</sub></td><td>F′(y<sup>∗</sup>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>G′(x<sup>∗</sup>)</td><td>−γ<sub>y</sub></td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <span class="matrix-bracket right" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div></div></div>
      <div class="equation teal" role="math">tr J = −(γ<sub>x</sub> + γ<sub>y</sub>) &lt; 0</div>
      <div class="equation " role="math">det J = γ<sub>x</sub>γ<sub>y</sub> − F′(y<sup>∗</sup>)G′(x<sup>∗</sup>)</div>
      <p>In two dimensions, Hopf requires a zero trace and positive determinant.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">Negative loop: F′G′ &lt; 0 gives det J &gt; 0. Double repression can instead lose stability at det J = 0.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 06 / 17</span><span>07</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 07; original slide 09 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-09" aria-labelledby="title-09" data-pdf-page="7" data-original-slide="9">
    <h2 id="title-09">The production function and the equilibrium</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div><p class="muted label">Production function</p><div class="equation " role="math">f(x) = α / (1 + x<sup>n</sup>) + α<sub>0</sub></div></div>
      <div><p class="muted label">Complete dynamics, with x<sub>0</sub> = x<sub>3</sub></p><div class="equation " role="math">dx<sub>i</sub>/dt = f(x<sub>i−1</sub>) − γx<sub>i</sub></div></div>
      <hr>
      <div class="two-columns compact"><div class="equation " role="math">x<sub>1</sub> = x<sub>2</sub> = x<sub>3</sub> = x<sup>∗</sup></div><div class="equation teal" role="math">f(x<sup>∗</sup>) = γx<sup>∗</sup></div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">Identical parameters and the symmetric steady state make the common slope g well defined.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 07 / 17</span><span>09</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 08; original slide 10 -->
  <section class="slide derivation" id="slide-10" aria-labelledby="title-10" data-pdf-page="8" data-original-slide="10">
    <h2 id="title-10">Linearization exposes gain and removal</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="equation " role="math">x<sub>i</sub> = x<sup>∗</sup> + u<sub>i</sub></div>
      <div class="equation " role="math">f(x<sup>∗</sup> + u<sub>i−1</sub>) ≈ f(x<sup>∗</sup>) + f′(x<sup>∗</sup>)u<sub>i−1</sub></div>
      <div class="equation small-equation" role="math">du<sub>i</sub>/dt ≈ [f(x<sup>∗</sup>) − γx<sup>∗</sup>] + f′(x<sup>∗</sup>)u<sub>i−1</sub> − γu<sub>i</sub></div>
      <p class="muted">The equilibrium terms cancel.</p>
      <div class="equation teal emphasis" role="math">du<sub>i</sub>/dt = −γu<sub>i</sub> − gu<sub>i−1</sub></div>
      <div class="equation small-equation" role="math">g = −f′(x<sup>∗</sup>) = αn(x<sup>∗</sup>)<sup>n−1</sup> / [1 + (x<sup>∗</sup>)<sup>n</sup>]<sup>2</sup> &gt; 0</div>

    </div>
    
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 08 / 17</span><span>10</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 09; original slide 11 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-11" aria-labelledby="title-11" data-pdf-page="9" data-original-slide="11">
    <h2 id="title-11">The Jacobian records the cyclic inhibition</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="equation " role="math">du/dt = Ju</div>
      <div class="two-columns matrix-layout"><div class="matrix-equation"><span>J =</span><div class="matrix-wrap" role="math" aria-label="Three by three Jacobian. Rows: minus gamma, zero, minus g; minus g, minus gamma, zero; zero, minus g, minus gamma.">
      <span class="matrix-bracket left" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <table class="matrix"><tbody>
        <tr><td>−γ</td><td>0</td><td>−g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>−g</td><td>−γ</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>−g</td><td>−γ</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <span class="matrix-bracket right" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div></div><div><h3 class="amber">Diagonal entries</h3><p>−γ removes each node’s deviation.</p><h3 class="teal">Cyclic off-diagonal entries</h3><p>−g transmits the upstream inhibitory response.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">u = (u<sub>1</sub>, u<sub>2</sub>, u<sub>3</sub>)<sup>T</sup>. The missing cross-links have zero derivatives.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 09 / 17</span><span>11</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 10; original slide 12 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-12" aria-labelledby="title-12" data-pdf-page="10" data-original-slide="12">
    <h2 id="title-12">The characteristic equation</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="equation " role="math">u(t) = v exp(λt) &nbsp; gives &nbsp; Jv = λv</div>
      <div class="matrix-equation characteristic"><span>λI − J =</span><div class="matrix-wrap" role="math" aria-label="Lambda I minus J. Rows: lambda plus gamma, zero, g; g, lambda plus gamma, zero; zero, g, lambda plus gamma.">
      <span class="matrix-bracket left" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <table class="matrix"><tbody>
        <tr><td>λ + γ</td><td>0</td><td>g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>g</td><td>λ + γ</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>g</td><td>λ + γ</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <span class="matrix-bracket right" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div></div>
      <div class="equation teal emphasis" role="math">det(λI − J) = (λ + γ)<sup>3</sup> + g<sup>3</sup> = 0</div>

    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">A nonzero v requires a zero determinant. The two surviving products come from the diagonal and the ring.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 10 / 17</span><span>12</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 11; original slide 13 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-13" aria-labelledby="title-13" data-pdf-page="11" data-original-slide="13">
    <h2 id="title-13">The complex cube roots supply the factor 2</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="equation " role="math">z = (λ + γ)/g &nbsp; gives &nbsp; z<sup>3</sup> = −1</div>
      <div class="equation " role="math">z<sub>1</sub> = −1, &nbsp; z<sub>2,3</sub> = 1/2 ± i√3/2</div>
      <hr>
      <div class="equation " role="math">λ<sub>1</sub> = −γ − g</div>
      <div class="equation teal emphasis" role="math">λ<sub>2,3</sub> = −γ + g/2 ± i√3g/2</div>

    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">The factor 2 comes from the real part 1/2 of the complex cube roots. Feedback must supply g/2 &gt; γ.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 11 / 17</span><span>13</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 12; original slide 14 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-14" aria-labelledby="title-14" data-pdf-page="12" data-original-slide="14">
    <h2 id="title-14">Real part controls growth, imaginary part controls frequency</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="equation " role="math">u(t) = A exp(at) cos(ωt + φ)</div>
      <div class="two-columns compact"><div class="equation teal" role="math">a = g/2 − γ</div><div class="equation blue" role="math">ω = √3g/2</div></div>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table ">
      <caption class="sr-only">Stability of the oscillatory mode</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Condition</th><th scope="col">a</th><th scope="col">Local response</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>g/γ &lt; 2</td><td>&lt; 0</td><td>All perturbations decay</td></tr>
      <tr><td>g/γ = 2</td><td>= 0</td><td>Candidate Hopf boundary</td></tr>
      <tr><td>g/γ &gt; 2</td><td>&gt; 0</td><td>Oscillatory perturbations grow</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
      <p>At onset: &nbsp; ω<sub>H</sub> = √3γ, &nbsp; T<sub>H</sub> = 2π/(√3γ)</p>

    </div>
    
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 12 / 17</span><span>14</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 13; original slide 15 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-15" aria-labelledby="title-15" data-pdf-page="13" data-original-slide="15">
    <h2 id="title-15">The oscillatory pair crosses the imaginary axis</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="chart-layout"><figure><svg class="plot" viewBox="0 0 650 390" role="img" aria-labelledby="eigen-title eigen-desc">
      <title id="eigen-title">Eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis</title>
      <desc id="eigen-desc">Gamma equals 1. For gains 1.5, 2.0 and 2.5, the real parts of the complex pair are -0.25, 0 and +0.25. The real eigenvalue is always negative.</desc>
      <line x1="43" y1="180" x2="618" y2="180" class="axis"></line><line x1="503" y1="30" x2="503" y2="330" class="axis"></line><line x1="43" y1="176" x2="43" y2="184" class="axis"></line><text x="43" y="203" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">-4</text><line x1="158" y1="176" x2="158" y2="184" class="axis"></line><text x="158" y="203" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">-3</text><line x1="273" y1="176" x2="273" y2="184" class="axis"></line><text x="273" y="203" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">-2</text><line x1="388" y1="176" x2="388" y2="184" class="axis"></line><text x="388" y="203" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">-1</text><line x1="503" y1="176" x2="503" y2="184" class="axis"></line><text x="503" y="203" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">0</text><line x1="618" y1="176" x2="618" y2="184" class="axis"></line><text x="618" y="203" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">1</text><line x1="499" y1="300" x2="507" y2="300" class="axis"></line><text x="514" y="305" text-anchor="start" class="tick">-2</text><line x1="499" y1="240" x2="507" y2="240" class="axis"></line><text x="514" y="245" text-anchor="start" class="tick">-1</text><line x1="499" y1="120" x2="507" y2="120" class="axis"></line><text x="514" y="125" text-anchor="start" class="tick">1</text><line x1="499" y1="60" x2="507" y2="60" class="axis"></line><text x="514" y="65" text-anchor="start" class="tick">2</text><text x="82" y="46" text-anchor="start" class="decay">Decay</text><text x="593" y="46" text-anchor="end" class="growth">Growth</text><text x="493" y="16" text-anchor="middle" class="axis-label">Im λ</text><text x="618" y="364" text-anchor="end" class="axis-label">Re λ</text><circle cx="215.500" cy="180.000" r="5.8" fill="#008786"><title>g=1.5, gamma=1; lambda=-2.500+0.000i</title></circle><circle cx="474.250" cy="102.058" r="5.8" fill="#008786"><title>g=1.5, gamma=1; lambda=-0.250+1.299i</title></circle><circle cx="474.250" cy="257.942" r="5.8" fill="#008786"><title>g=1.5, gamma=1; lambda=-0.250-1.299i</title></circle><circle cx="158.000" cy="180.000" r="5.8" fill="#b36a00"><title>g=2.0, gamma=1; lambda=-3.000+0.000i</title></circle><circle cx="503.000" cy="76.077" r="5.8" fill="#b36a00"><title>g=2.0, gamma=1; lambda=0.000+1.732i</title></circle><circle cx="503.000" cy="283.923" r="5.8" fill="#b36a00"><title>g=2.0, gamma=1; lambda=0.000-1.732i</title></circle><circle cx="100.500" cy="180.000" r="5.8" fill="#c74b20"><title>g=2.5, gamma=1; lambda=-3.500+0.000i</title></circle><circle cx="531.750" cy="50.096" r="5.8" fill="#c74b20"><title>g=2.5, gamma=1; lambda=0.250+2.165i</title></circle><circle cx="531.750" cy="309.904" r="5.8" fill="#c74b20"><title>g=2.5, gamma=1; lambda=0.250-2.165i</title></circle>
    </svg></figure><div class="chart-explanation eigen-legend"><p><strong>γ = 1.0 in each example</strong></p><div><h3 class="teal">g = 1.5</h3><p>Re λ<sub>2,3</sub> = −0.25</p></div><div><h3 class="amber">g = 2.0</h3><p>Re λ<sub>2,3</sub> = 0</p></div><div><h3 class="orange">g = 2.5</h3><p>Re λ<sub>2,3</sub> = +0.25</p></div></div></div>

    </div>
    <p class="slide-note">At fixed γ, increasing g moves the pair right. At fixed g, decreasing γ also moves it right.</p>
    <footer class="slide-footer"><span class="source-page">PDF 13 / 17</span><span>15</span></footer>
  </section>
  <!-- PDF page 14; original slide 16 -->
  <section class="slide " id="slide-16" aria-labelledby="title-16" data-pdf-page="14" data-original-slide="16">
    <h2 id="title-16">Large g means a steep local repression slope</h2>
    <div class="slide-content">

      <div class="chart-layout"><figure><svg class="plot" viewBox="0 0 660 380" role="img" aria-labelledby="hill-title hill-desc">
